{
  "verb": "backlog",
  "why": "Compacting a long conversation is a stretch of a turn in which nothing reaches the room and new messages go unanswered, for minutes. The room has to be able to say it is compacting rather than leave a silence that reads as a hung session, and to say when it is over. Claude Code reports both ends as system/status records; a status record about anything else (a permission mode) says nothing to the room. Codex reports a contextCompaction item that starts and completes. pi says it only on its live event stream (compaction_start / compaction_end); its cell is the records its runner writes into the log for them.",
